  This dataset is publicly available for research. The details are described in [Cortez et al., 2009]. 
  Please include this citation if you plan to use this database:

  P. Cortez, A. Cerdeira, F. Almeida, T. Matos and J. Reis. 
  Modeling wine preferences by data mining from physicochemical properties.
  Decision Support Systems 47(4):547–553, 2009. ISSN: 0167-9236.
  DOI: http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.dss.2009.05.016
  Pre-press (pdf): http://www3.dsi.uminho.pt/pcortez/winequality09.pdf
  bib: http://www3.dsi.uminho.pt/pcortez/dss09.bib

Title: Wine Quality 

Sources
   Created by: Paulo Cortez (Univ. Minho), Antonio Cerdeira, Fernando Almeida, Telmo Matos and Jose Reis (CVRVV) @ 2009
   
  P. Cortez, A. Cerdeira, F. Almeida, T. Matos and J. Reis. 
  Modeling wine preferences by data mining from physicochemical properties.
  Decision Support Systems 47(4):547–553, 2009.

  In the above reference, two datasets were created, using red and white wine samples.
  The inputs include objective lab tests (e.g., pH) and the output is based on sensory data
  (median of at least 3 evaluations made by wine experts). Each expert graded wine quality 
  between 0 (very bad) and 10 (very excellent).
 
Relevant Information:

   The two datasets are related to red and white variants of the Portuguese \"Vinho Verde\" wine.
   For more details, consult: http://www.vinhoverde.pt/en/ or the reference [Cortez et al., 2009].
   Due to privacy and logistic issues, only physicochemical (inputs) and sensory (the output) variables 
   are available (e.g., there is no data about grape types, wine brand, wine selling price, etc.).


Number of Attributes: 11 input + 1 output attribute
  
   Note: several of the attributes may be correlated; feature selection can be useful.

Attribute Information:

   For more information, read [Cortez et al., 2009].

   Input variables (based on physicochemical tests) — with plain‑English explanations:

   1 - fixed acidity (g tartaric acid / L): non‑volatile acids that do not evaporate easily. They drive the wine’s basic tart, fresh taste and overall structure.
   2 - volatile acidity (g acetic acid / L): acids that evaporate readily, mainly acetic (vinegar) acid. High values can make wine smell/taste vinegary or like nail polish remover.
   3 - citric acid (g / L): a natural acid present in small amounts (sometimes added). It brightens flavors and can make the wine taste fresher.
   4 - residual sugar (g / L): natural grape sugar left after fermentation stops. More residual sugar means a sweeter‑tasting wine.
   5 - chlorides (g sodium chloride / L): a measure of salt content. Small differences can subtly affect body and taste, sometimes reflecting soil or maritime influence.
   6 - free sulfur dioxide (mg / L): the portion of SO₂ that is active as a preservative. It protects wine from oxidation and spoilage microbes; effectiveness depends strongly on pH.
   7 - total sulfur dioxide (mg / L): all forms of SO₂ present (free + bound). Indicates overall sulfite level used for preservation and is often regulated.
   8 - density (g / cm³): how heavy the liquid is for its volume. It reflects dissolved substances—higher with sugar and lower with alcohol.
   9 - pH (unitless): a scale of acidity; lower pH = more acidic. It influences color (especially in red wines), stability, and how well SO₂ works.
   10 - sulphates (g potassium sulphate / L): sulfate salts measured as K₂SO₄. They are linked to wine preservation and can affect the perception of bitterness/structure.
   11 - alcohol (% vol): percentage of ethanol by volume. Higher alcohol increases body, warmth and can amplify aromas.

   Output variable (based on sensory data): 
   12 - quality (score between 0 and 10)

Missing Attribute Values: None